# Shell scripts run inside Linux containers and on CI runners. With
# core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout would hand them to /bin/sh with CRLF
# line endings, which fails as "bad interpreter".
*.sh text eol=lf

# Unity resolves assets by the GUID in the .meta file. Treating these as text
# keeps diffs reviewable; forcing LF keeps the GUIDs byte-identical whatever
# platform wrote them.
*.meta text eol=lf
*.asmdef text eol=lf
*.shader text eol=lf
*.cginc text eol=lf

# The documentation figures are generated and then compared byte for byte
# against the generator's output in CI. A CRLF checkout would fail that check
# on Windows even with nothing changed.
*.svg text eol=lf

# Data files that shell parses field by field: packages.lock inside the fetch
# container, ProjectVersion.txt through sed on the host. A CRLF checkout leaves
# a trailing \r on the last field of every line, so the parse fails with an
# unrelated-looking error ("wget: bad address ''") instead of a readable one.
*.lock text eol=lf
ProjectVersion.txt text eol=lf
